<h1>Database design (D1)</h1>
<p class="lede">Every table, column, key and index of the Cloud edition's database, why it exists, and how changes to the structure are rolled out.</p>

<div class="callout eli5"><span class="callout-title">Explained simply</span>A relational database is a set of spreadsheets (tables). Each row is one thing (a user, a session). A column that points to a row in another table (like <code>user_id</code>) is a <em>foreign key</em>. "ON DELETE CASCADE" means: when the user row is deleted, every row pointing to it is deleted too, automatically.</div>

<h2>Entity-relationship diagram</h2>
<script type="application/json" class="diagram" data-kind="flow" data-wide="true" data-caption="All tables. Every user-owned table points to users(id) with ON DELETE CASCADE.">
{"w": 940, "h": 600, "wide": true,
 "nodes": [
  {"id": "users", "x": 360, "y": 230, "w": 220, "title": "users", "kind": "db", "lines": ["id  PK (random uuid)", "atlassian_account_id  UNIQUE", "name, email, picture", "created_at, last_seen_at", "profile_at"]},
  {"id": "sessions", "x": 20, "y": 20, "w": 230, "title": "sessions", "kind": "db", "lines": ["id_hash  PK (sha256 of cookie)", "user_id  FK", "created_at, expires_at", "last_used_at, user_agent"]},
  {"id": "grants", "x": 280, "y": 20, "w": 250, "title": "oauth_grants", "kind": "db", "lines": ["user_id  PK, FK", "enc_tokens (AES-GCM)", "key_id, access_expires_at", "scopes, version, lease_until", "status: active | revoked"]},
  {"id": "sites", "x": 560, "y": 20, "w": 190, "title": "jira_sites", "kind": "db", "lines": ["user_id  FK", "cloud_id", "name, url", "PK (user_id, cloud_id)"]},
  {"id": "settings", "x": 20, "y": 230, "w": 300, "title": "user_settings", "kind": "db", "lines": ["user_id  PK, FK", "active_cloud_id", "severity_fields, issue_types", "severity_ids, severity_names", "monitor_enabled, monitor_paused", "interval_min, closed_window_days", "slot (0-59), notify_prefs (JSON)"]},
  {"id": "state", "x": 620, "y": 210, "w": 300, "title": "monitor_state", "kind": "db", "lines": ["user_id  PK, FK", "cloud_id, initialized", "snapshot (JSON), seen (JSON)", "status_cats, priority_names", "checked_at, reconciled_at", "deferred, failures, backoff_until", "last_error"]},
  {"id": "events", "x": 20, "y": 430, "w": 300, "title": "events (notification history)", "kind": "db", "lines": ["id  PK autoincrement", "user_id  FK", "type, at, occurred_at", "issue_key, summary, project, url", "actor, actor_id, from_v, to_v, detail", "escalated, importance, own, read", "pushed: 0 pending | 1 done | 2 gave up"]},
  {"id": "push", "x": 360, "y": 450, "w": 240, "title": "push_subscriptions", "kind": "db", "lines": ["id  PK", "user_id  FK", "endpoint  UNIQUE", "p256dh, auth, label", "created_at, last_ok_at, failures"]},
  {"id": "pdr", "x": 640, "y": 450, "w": 140, "title": "pdr_reports", "kind": "db", "lines": ["account_id  PK", "reported_at"]},
  {"id": "meta", "x": 800, "y": 450, "w": 120, "title": "app_meta", "kind": "db", "lines": ["key  PK", "value"]},
  {"id": "rl", "x": 800, "y": 20, "w": 120, "title": "rate_limits", "kind": "db", "lines": ["bucket  PK", "count", "expires_at"]}
 ],
 "edges": [
  {"from": "sessions", "to": "users"}, {"from": "grants", "to": "users"}, {"from": "sites", "to": "users"},
  {"from": "settings", "to": "users"}, {"from": "state", "to": "users"}, {"from": "events", "to": "users"}, {"from": "push", "to": "users"}
 ]}
</script>

<h2>Table by table</h2>
<h3><code>users</code></h3>
<p>One row per Atlassian account that has signed in. <code>id</code> is a random UUID (internal, never shown); <code>atlassian_account_id</code> is unique and is how a returning user is recognised. <code>profile_at</code> records when name/email/picture were fetched, for the privacy reports.</p>

<h3><code>sessions</code></h3>
<p>A browser that is signed in. The primary key is the SHA-256 hash of the cookie value, so the database alone cannot be used to impersonate anyone. Expiry is checked on every request (30 days absolute, 7 days idle); expired rows are deleted hourly. Index on <code>user_id</code> (sign out everywhere) and <code>expires_at</code> (cleanup).</p>

<h3><code>oauth_grants</code></h3>
<p>The user's Atlassian tokens, encrypted. One per user because Atlassian issues one grant per app per account. <code>version</code> and <code>lease_until</code> implement safe refresh-token rotation. <code>status</code> becomes <code>revoked</code> when Atlassian refuses a refresh.</p>

<h3><code>jira_sites</code></h3>
<p>Sites the grant can reach, from <code>accessible-resources</code>. Composite primary key <code>(user_id, cloud_id)</code>. Replaced on each sign-in.</p>

<h3><code>user_settings</code></h3>
<p>Per-user preferences and monitor schedule. <code>slot</code> (0–59, random at sign-up) spreads users across minutes: a user is due when <code>(minute − slot) mod interval_min = 0</code>. <code>notify_prefs</code> holds the notification rules the server applies to pushes (event types, quiet hours, time zone, frequency, push on/off). <code>severity_ids</code> caches resolved custom field ids (<code>["none"]</code> means "looked up, nothing found").</p>

<h3><code>monitor_state</code></h3>
<p>The JARVIS checkpoint for the active site:</p>
<ul>
  <li><code>snapshot</code>: <code>{"ABC-1": ["2026-11-18T10:00:00.000+0530", "Login fails", "ABC"], …}</code> (updated time, summary, project for each assigned bug)</li>
  <li><code>seen</code>: up to 3000 ids of changelog items and comments already turned into events</li>
  <li><code>status_cats</code>: status id → category, learned lazily</li>
  <li><code>checked_at</code>: last fully processed check (heartbeat every 20 minutes when idle)</li>
  <li><code>reconciled_at</code>: last full comparison (every 6 hours)</li>
  <li><code>deferred</code>, <code>failures</code>, <code>backoff_until</code>, <code>last_error</code>: scheduling and error handling</li>
</ul>

<h3><code>events</code></h3>
<p>Notification history, kept 30 days. <code>own</code> marks your own actions (recorded, never pushed, stored as read). <code>pushed</code> tracks delivery so failures are retried: 0 pending, 1 delivered or nothing to deliver, 2 gave up after 6 hours. <code>actor_id</code> lets the privacy job find and scrub names of people whose accounts closed. Indexes: <code>(user_id, id)</code> for history pages, a partial index <code>WHERE pushed = 0</code> for the retry sweep, and a partial index on <code>actor_id</code>.</p>

<h3><code>push_subscriptions</code></h3>
<p>One per browser/device that enabled background notifications (max 5 per user). <code>endpoint</code> is unique: if another account subscribes the same browser (shared computer), the subscription moves.</p>

<h3><code>rate_limits</code>, <code>pdr_reports</code>, <code>app_meta</code></h3>
<p>Fixed-window counters for sign-in attempts (bucket = hashed IP + 10-minute window); when each account id was last reported to Atlassian; small app-wide values (reporting cycle, back-off).</p>

<h2>Important queries</h2>
<h3>Who is due this minute (one query for all users)</h3>
<pre><code>SELECT s.user_id, u.atlassian_account_id, s.active_cloud_id, j.url, …, g.enc_tokens, …, st.snapshot, st.seen, …
FROM user_settings s
JOIN users u        ON u.id = s.user_id
JOIN oauth_grants g ON g.user_id = s.user_id AND g.status = 'active'
JOIN jira_sites j   ON j.user_id = s.user_id AND j.cloud_id = s.active_cloud_id
LEFT JOIN monitor_state st ON st.user_id = s.user_id
WHERE s.monitor_enabled = 1 AND s.monitor_paused = 0 AND COALESCE(st.backoff_until, 0) &lt;= :now
  AND (COALESCE(st.deferred, 0) = 1 OR ((:minute - s.slot) % s.interval_min) = 0)
ORDER BY COALESCE(st.deferred, 0) DESC, ((s.slot * 7919 + :minute) % 1009)
LIMIT 60</code></pre>
<p>Everything a check needs (token, site, snapshot, settings) arrives in this single row, saving subrequests. The odd ordering formula rotates who goes first, so no user is always last.</p>

<h3>Atomic sign-in</h3>
<p><code>finishLogin()</code> sends the user upsert, grant upsert, site replacement, settings upsert and session insert as one <code>DB.batch()</code>. D1 runs a batch as a transaction: all succeed or none do.</p>

<h3>Safe rotation</h3>
<pre><code>UPDATE oauth_grants SET lease_until = :now+30s
WHERE user_id = :u AND version = :v AND lease_until &lt; :now RETURNING version;
-- … refresh at Atlassian …
UPDATE oauth_grants SET enc_tokens = :new, version = version + 1, lease_until = 0
WHERE user_id = :u AND version = :v;</code></pre>

<h2>Migrations</h2>
<p>The structure is defined by numbered SQL files in <code>cloud/migrations/</code>:</p>
<ul>
  <li><code>0001_init.sql</code>: all tables above (except the reporting ones).</li>
  <li><code>0002_personal_data_reporting.sql</code>: <code>users.profile_at</code>, <code>events.actor_id</code>, <code>pdr_reports</code>, <code>app_meta</code>.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>npx wrangler d1 migrations apply DB --local --env dev          # your computer
npx wrangler d1 migrations apply DB --remote --env production  # Cloudflare</code></pre>
<p>D1 records applied migrations in its own table, so re-running applies only new files. Rules: never edit a migration that already ran in production; add a new numbered file instead; prefer additive changes (new columns with defaults) so old and new code can run side by side during a deploy.</p>

<h2>Write budget</h2>
<p>D1's free plan allows 100,000 rows written per day. An idle check writes nothing; a heartbeat writes one row every 20 minutes (~72/day/user); each event writes a row (plus index entries); token refreshes write about 24/day/user. Roughly 100–150 rows per active user per day, so about 700–1000 users before writes become the limit, well beyond the CPU limit (~60 users). See <a href="cf-limits.html">The free tier and capacity maths</a>.</p>
